{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Split runner.py along a call-graph seam — it sits at exactly the 800-LOC hard cap\n\n## Problem\n\n`src/rebar/llm/runner.py` is **exactly 800 LOC** (`wc -l`), which is the repo hard cap in\n`.github/module-size-limit.txt`. The CI module-size gate compares with `$1 > lim` (strictly greater), so 800\nPASSES today — but there is **zero headroom**: the next line added to this file fails `Verified` for everyone.\n\nThis is a pre-existing condition, not caused by one story: runner.py was 783 before `7468`, 787 before `dc0d`.\n`AGENTS.md` targets **200–500 LOC** per file, so it is well past target.\n\nIt is now a **hard blocker, not a nicety**. Both remaining code stories in epic `061c` must ADD lines to this\nfile and cannot:\n\n- `2932` (Bedrock) adds a `supports_temperature` capability check plus the cache-zero warning;\n- `343b` (verdict provenance) adds provenance capture.\n\nBoth are strictly positive LOC, so there is no net-neutral way to write them. This task is recorded as `blocks`\non both.\n\n## Approach — the seam, chosen from measured data\n\n`AGENTS.md`: split only along call-graph seams that already exist, never mechanically to hit a number, and never\ncreate a file under 100 LOC. Measured top-level symbol sizes in the current file:\n\n| symbol | lines |\n|---|---|\n| `PydanticAIRunner` | 400 |\n| `_pai_structured` | 76 |\n| `RunRequest` | 47 |\n| `FakeRunner` | 44 |\n| `_extract_usage` | 34 |\n| `_pai_check_config` | 29 |\n| `_readonly_gate` | 21 |\n| `get_runner` / `Runner` / `_check_tool_capability` | 19 / 17 / 17 |\n| `_warn_if_zeroed_usage` | 16 |\n| `_import_pydantic_ai` | 13 |\n| `effective_max_tokens` / `effective_max_iterations` | 10 / 9 |\n| `_FAULTY_OUTPUT_SNIPPET_CHARS` | 3 |\n\n**Extract the structured-output execution cluster** into a new leaf `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py`:\n\n- `_pai_structured` (76) — the bounded structured-output retry driver\n- `_extract_usage` (34) — which `_pai_structured` calls twice\n- `_warn_if_zeroed_usage` (16) — which interprets what `_extract_usage` returns\n- `effective_max_iterations` (9) + `effective_max_tokens` (10) — the per-request budget sizing the retry loop\n  and `run()` both enforce\n- `_FAULTY_OUTPUT_SNIPPET_CHARS` (3) — used only by `_pai_structured`\n\n**= 148 lines moved.** `runner.py` lands at ~657 (under the 700 target below, with real headroom), and\n`structured_run.py` is ~165–175 LOC with its docstring and imports — comfortably over the 100-LOC floor.\n\n**Why this is a genuine seam, with the dependency list enumerated by AST rather than by eye.** An earlier draft\nof this plan claimed the cluster references \"exactly `_extract_usage`, `_FAULTY_OUTPUT_SNIPPET_CHARS`, and\n`RunRequest`\". That was produced by a hand-picked grep and was **incomplete** — it missed `usage_log`. Redone\nproperly by walking the AST of each cluster member and intersecting its free names with runner.py's module-level\nnames, the complete set each member needs is:\n\n| cluster member | module-level names it needs |\n|---|---|\n| `_pai_structured` | `RunRequest`, `StructuredOutputError`, `UnretryableOutputError`, `_FAULTY_OUTPUT_SNIPPET_CHARS`, `_extract_usage`, `usage_log` |\n| `_warn_if_zeroed_usage` | `logger` |\n| `_extract_usage`, `effective_max_iterations`, `effective_max_tokens` | none |\n\nWhich resolves cleanly, and only ONE item is a runner-defined dependency:\n\n- `_extract_usage` and `_FAULTY_OUTPUT_SNIPPET_CHARS` **move with the cluster** — not external at all.\n- `StructuredOutputError` / `UnretryableOutputError` come from `rebar.llm.errors` (verified: both defined there).\n- `usage_log` is `from rebar.llm import usage_log` — a top-level import the new module must carry. Verified it\n  pulls in NO heavy module, so the stdlib-only `import rebar.llm` contract holds.\n- `logger` is a fresh `logging.getLogger(__name__)` in the new module.\n- `RunRequest` is the only symbol still owned by `runner`, and it is used **only in an annotation** — see below.\n\nThe cohesion story holds: \"drive a structured-output run and account its token usage\". The members call each\nother, and nothing else in the file calls into the middle of them.\n\n**The one snag, and its resolution.** `_pai_structured`'s signature annotates `req: RunRequest`, and `RunRequest`\nstays in `runner.py` (moving it would churn every `from rebar.llm.runner import RunRequest` site across the\ntests for no benefit). With `from __future__ import annotations` the annotation is a string at runtime, so\n`structured_run.py` imports `RunRequest` under `if TYPE_CHECKING:` only. There is therefore **no runtime import\nfrom `runner`**, which is what the leaf rule actually protects against — matching the convention already used by\n`anthropic_model.py`, `providers.py` and `capabilities.py`.\n\n`runner.py` imports the five moved callables from `structured_run`. Direction is runner -> structured_run, never\nback.\n\n**Rejected alternatives.** (a) Moving `_pai_check_config` (29) to `config.py` — the obvious quick win, but\n`config.py` is 768 LOC so it would land at ~796 and merely relocate the same landmine. (b) A tiny module holding\nonly `_extract_usage` + `_warn_if_zeroed_usage` (50 lines) — violates the sub-100-LOC split rule. (c) Splitting\n`PydanticAIRunner.run()` itself (the real 400-line bulk) — a much larger behavioural risk, and not needed to\nclear the cap; worth its own story if runner.py trends up again.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py` (**new leaf**) — the five moved symbols.\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — the symbols removed and imported instead.\n- Test import sites for the moved symbols. **Enumerate them with a symbol-reference query, not by eye**, and\n  sweep separately for string-literal patch targets (`monkeypatch.setattr(runner_mod, \"_pai_structured\", ...)`)\n  which an LSP cannot resolve — that exact class broke `7468` and `f184` in this epic. Known at time of writing:\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_execution_mode_dispatch.py` patches `runner_mod._pai_structured` by string.\n\n## Testing\n\n**This is a pure relocation with no behaviour change, so it cannot be RED-first, and pretending otherwise would\nbe dishonest.** The oracle is the existing suite: 7517 tests currently pass, and they must still pass with no\nedits other than moved import sites and patch targets. A behaviour change here shows up as a failure in tests\nwritten for the behaviour, which is exactly the regression net this split needs.\n\nThe only genuinely new assertion is the structural one: that `structured_run.py` has no runtime import from\n`runner`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] **`runner.py` is under 700 LOC** (projected ~657) — real headroom, not sitting on the cap — and\n      `structured_run.py` is under 500 (the `AGENTS.md` target ceiling). Scoped to the two files this task\n      touches: an earlier draft said \"no file under `src/rebar` exceeds 700\", which was unpassable, because\n      `config.py` is 768 LOC and is deliberately NOT touched here (see rejected alternative (a)). `config.py`\n      passes the real 800 gate with ~32 lines of headroom, so it is a known condition rather than this task's\n      work; splitting it needs its own seam analysis if it ever trends up.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py` is >= 100 LOC (the `AGENTS.md` floor for a split)\n- [ ] `structured_run.py` has NO runtime import from `runner`: `grep -n 'from rebar.llm.runner\\|import runner'\n      src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py` shows only a `TYPE_CHECKING`-guarded line, if any\n- [ ] `import rebar.llm` still pulls in NO heavy module (httpx / anthropic / pydantic_ai / openai / boto3),\n      verified empirically — heavy imports stay function-local\n- [ ] the FULL gating suite passes with **7517 passed** and zero test-logic edits: only import sites and\n      string-literal patch targets may change, proving behaviour preservation\n- [ ] every moved symbol's reference sites were found via a symbol-reference query PLUS a string-literal grep,\n      and both were run (the string-literal sweep is what an LSP structurally cannot do)\n- [ ] `structured_run.py` carries `from rebar.llm import usage_log` and imports its two error types from\n      `rebar.llm.errors` — the AST-enumerated dependency set above, with nothing left implicit\n- [ ] [operator-attested] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the FULL `make test` pass, evidenced by the Gerrit\n      change id and its `Verified +1` recorded in a comment on this ticket (this proof is external to the repo)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785392313334838001,"uuid":"29de3673-711d-42d9-bcef-17812e6f26a8"}